INSERT INTO ... SELECT can populate a table from query results. This complete independent example copies distinct ID/name pairs.
```sql
CREATE TABLE raw_items(category_id INTEGER,category_name TEXT);
CREATE TABLE categories(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,name TEXT);
INSERT INTO raw_items VALUES(10,'paper'),(10,'paper'),(20,'tools');
INSERT INTO categories(id,name)
SELECT DISTINCT category_id,category_name
FROM raw_items;
SELECT id,name
FROM categories
ORDER BY id;
```
The result is 10/paper and 20/tools; the repeated source pair becomes one category.

This complete example updates an existing key and inserts a missing one without removing the unrelated preference.
```sql
CREATE TABLE demo_settings(key TEXT PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL,value TEXT NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO demo_settings VALUES('sound','on'),('font','large');
INSERT INTO demo_settings(key,value) VALUES('sound','off') ON CONFLICT(key) DO UPDATE SET value=excluded.value;
INSERT INTO demo_settings(key,value) VALUES('alerts','on') ON CONFLICT(key) DO UPDATE SET value=excluded.value;
SELECT key,value
FROM demo_settings
ORDER BY key;
```
The rows are alerts/on, font/large and sound/off.

